"Thin Lenses and the Depth of an Image | VirtualDemos",
"Thin Lenses and the Depth of an Image",
"<strong>Where it is in the demo:</strong> the Optics Bench, with the Converging lens, A shape, point by point and A shape in a magnifier setups. The magnifier, microscope and telescope have their own worksheet, Magnifiers, Microscopes and Telescopes.",
"<strong>Using the demo.</strong> An object arrow sends rays through one or two lenses. Click a lens to open its editor: converging or diverging, focal length and aperture. Drag an element to move it; with it selected, the arrow keys move it {#} cm, or {#} cm with Shift. With one lens, the Image section gives the object distance, the image distance and magnification found from the traced rays, and the image’s kind.",
"<strong>What the demo models.</strong> Each lens is an ideal thin lens: it bends every ray by the thin lens rule exactly, so it has no aberrations, and its traced images agree with the thin lens equation. A shape source (a flat house, letter F or square lying in the plane of the drawing) is imaged point by point: five rays from each point of its outline, and the image point where they meet; the Shape and its image section gives the object’s depth along the axis (with the object distances of its near and far ends), the traced image depth, their ratio, \\(m_1m_2\\) from the lens equation at the two ends, and the traced height ratio of the tallest corner. A shape reading records these; a series of shape readings goes to the Depth table. Simulated data.",
"Measure where thin lenses form images, focus a camera, and measure how an image is stretched along the axis.",
"A thin lens of focal length \\(f\\) (\\(f > 0\\) converging, \\(f < 0\\) diverging) forms the image of an object a distance \\(d_o\\) away at \\(d_i\\), with",
"\\[\\frac{1}{d_o} + \\frac{1}{d_i} = \\frac{1}{f}, \\qquad m = -\\frac{d_i}{d_o}.\\]",
"\\(d_i > 0\\) is a real image on the far side of the lens; \\(d_i < 0\\) is a virtual image on the object’s side. A camera is a converging lens that makes a real image on a sensor; to focus on a nearer object, the lens moves farther from the sensor.",
"<strong>Depth of an image.</strong> A lens makes images of points at different distances at different places, so an object with depth along the axis has an image with depth too. For two points of the object at \\(d_{o1}\\) and \\(d_{o2}\\), with magnifications \\(m_1\\) and \\(m_2\\), the thin lens equation gives exactly",
"\\[\\frac{\\lvert d_{i2}-d_{i1}\\rvert}{\\lvert d_{o2}-d_{o1}\\rvert} = m_1m_2,\\]",
"close to \\(m^2\\) for a thin object. So an image is stretched or squeezed along the axis by about the square of its height magnification.",
"A converging lens has \\(f = 10\\) cm. Find the image distance and magnification for an object {#} cm from it, and for one {#} cm from it. Describe each image.",
"A diverging lens has \\(f = -10\\) cm. Find the image distance and magnification for an object {#} cm from it.",
"A converging lens makes a sharp picture of an object on a screen {#} cm behind the lens. If the screen is moved {#} cm farther from the lens while the object and lens stay where they are, does the picture stay sharp and get larger, stay sharp and keep its size, or go blurred? Describe what you expect and why.",
"There are no hazards in the simulation. Never look at the Sun through a lens or camera viewfinder.",
"<strong>Part {#}: converging lens.</strong> Choose the setup Converging lens (\\(f = 10\\) cm; the object starts {#} cm from the lens). Click the object arrow and move it with the arrow keys ({#} cm a press) until the Object distance dₒ readout in the Image section is about {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm, and press Record reading at each. Export to worksheet, then Clear.",
"<strong>Part {#}: focusing a camera.</strong> With the object about {#} cm from the lens, click the screen and move it with the arrow keys until the picture on it is sharpest; read the distance from the lens to the screen on the {#} cm grid and write it under the Part {#} table (the table has no notes column). While the picture is at its sharpest, press the right arrow key {#} more times to move the screen {#} cm farther from the lens and watch the picture; then move the screen back, and write what you saw in your answer to Question {#}. Repeat the focusing with the object about {#} cm away. (Nothing new is recorded.)",
"<strong>Part {#}: diverging lens.</strong> Click the lens and press Diverging in its editor (the Focal length slider stays at {#} cm, so \\(f = -10\\) cm). Move the object to about {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm from the lens and record at each. Export to worksheet, then Clear.",
"<strong>Part {#}: depth of an image.</strong> Choose the setup A shape, point by point (\\(f = 8\\) cm). Click the house and move it until its middle (halfway between the two dₒ values that the Object depth along the axis readout gives for its ends) is about {#}, {#}, {#} and {#} cm from the lens, and record at each. Then choose A shape in a magnifier and record it as it opens. Send this series; it goes to the Depth table.",
